<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	MACOS-SIGN-NOTARIZE: this app ships WITHOUT com.apple.security.app-sandbox, so these are
	plain hardened-runtime entitlements, not App Sandbox grants — Developer ID distribution
	(outside the Mac App Store) does not require sandboxing, and this app can't be sandboxed
	without breaking real functionality: it spawns arbitrary child processes via a PTY
	(packages/app/src-tauri/src/pty.rs, an interactive shell for the user), shells out to
	`git`/`open` (commands.rs), and talks to the chimerad daemon over a Unix domain socket
	(daemon.rs) at a user-chosen CHIMERA_HOME path — none of which fit the sandbox's fixed
	container/entitlement model cleanly.

	Kept, justified:
	- com.apple.security.device.audio-input: push-to-talk voice input (packages/app/src/voice/
	  audioCapture.ts) records via getUserMedia in the WKWebView. Actual access is gated by
	  Info.plist's NSMicrophoneUsageDescription + the user's TCC consent regardless of this
	  entitlement; keeping it documents the real capability and costs nothing extra to ship.

	Deliberately NOT added (each would silently widen the attack surface as an app-store-style
	permissive default, and none are required by anything in this codebase):
	- com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory: no JIT / self-modifying
	  code in this process. Tauri's WKWebView content (incl. any JS JIT) runs out-of-process in
	  Apple's own signed com.apple.WebKit.WebContent, which isn't covered by our entitlements.
	- com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: this app loads no third-party/unsigned
	  dylibs or plugins at runtime.
	- com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables: not needed; nothing here depends on
	  DYLD_* injection.
	- com.apple.security.app-sandbox and its device/file-access companions: see above — would
	  break child-process spawning (PTY shell, git, open) and the daemon socket connection.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
